  "risks": [
    {
      "id": "R001",
      "category": "regulatory-compliance",
      "description": "SingPass/MyInfo integration requires SNDGO/GovTech approval and API access, named as an external dependency in BRD §17.2. Lead time is outside both NTUC Club's and PALO IT's control, and SingPass is the mandated baseline enrolment path. If approval slips, the primary front door to the programme is unavailable at go-live.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Confirm application status and expected lead time with NTUC HQ / Group IT immediately. Design the enrolment flow so the non-SingPass paths (Google, Apple, email, mobile OTP) can carry launch alone, with SingPass added without redesign. Log the fallback explicitly in the compliance response.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["identity", "critical-path", "go-live"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R002",
      "category": "operational",
      "description": "Approximately 15 weeks remain to the 1 December 2026 go-live (as at 2026-08-17). The member surface as it stands in apps/pwa is a 607-line skeleton: no membership card, no voucher wallet, no profile, no consent screen, no parking entitlement, no promotions surface, no notification handling. Nine of the twelve Phase 1 member features in the brief do not exist in any form.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "high",
      "mitigation": "Sequence discovery so that the card (f1) and enrolment (f2) are the first to reach design certainty — they gate every other journey. Keep research activities short and parallel. Escalate early if the discovery timeline cannot compress enough to leave adequate build time. RA-10 (2026-08-17) adds a qualifier: the skeleton is not merely incomplete, its existing accessibility and error-handling patterns are ones that should not be carried forward — treat reuse as a decision, not a default.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["timeline", "go-live", "scope"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R003",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "No user research of any kind exists. The three customer segments (Families, PMEs, DINKs/Silvers) are asserted in BRD §3.2 without sizing, behavioural data or needs analysis. Every member-facing design decision currently rests on segment labels. The risk is a launch built to a plausible but wrong model of who uses the club and what they want from it.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "high",
      "mitigation": "Recruit against a behavioural grid (regular / occasional / lapsed / rare-or-never) with the three named segments recorded as a post-hoc classification, so the segmentation can be falsified rather than restated — see the RA-01 screener instrument (01-research/discussion-guides/RA-01-participant-screener.md, 2026-08-17). Front-load JTBD interviews, including a deliberate union-member cohort. Where the timeline forbids research before a decision, record the decision as an assumption in this log rather than treating it as settled.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["research", "segments", "evidence"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R004",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "BR-P1-007 mandates identical rebate yield across both tiers — tier differentiation comes only from redemption value and ancillary benefits. A union member auto-placed into Tier 2 therefore earns no faster than a Tier 1 member. If Tier 2 does not read as genuine recognition, the tier is cosmetic, the 'Premium' label is hollow, and the Phase 2 tier-migration KPI has no foundation. CORROBORATED by RA-07 (2026-08-17): no Singapore comparator runs a named status tier with neither an earn nor a redemption differential — NTUC Phase 1 would be the first. Tier 2 will also be the majority tier, holding both the lowest first-upgrade threshold in the comparator set ($1,500 vs Changi S$4,000 and FRx $8,000) and union auto-placement.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "high",
      "mitigation": "Adopt a Tier 2 redemption-value uplift (1,000 points = $10 at Tier 2, $5 at Tier 1), which resolves R004 and I004 together, is exactly the mechanism BR-P1-007 prescribes, is demonstrated in-market by FRx, and is already specified in BRD §11.4 — merely assigned to Tier 3 in Phase 2. Test the tier proposition directly in JTBD interviews with union members. Design the member surface so Tier 2's actual differentiators (day passes, D'Resort discount, early-bird booking, 2-hour parking, 3x birthday multiplier) are visible as a standing entitlement rather than discovered incidentally.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["tiering", "proposition", "union-members"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R005",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "The solutioning architecture places the member and integration layer on Azure and the Open Loyalty engine on AWS, joined by a site-to-site IPsec VPN described as 'the only cross-cloud, latency-sensitive hop: earn, redeem, balance check, enrolment sync'. Every latency-critical member action crosses it. NFR-003 requires member-facing actions to complete within a proposed SLA under peak load (NFR-002: public and school holiday footfall).",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Establish the latency budget for card display, balance check and redemption before UI design commits to synchronous patterns. Consider read-through caching of balance and tier on the Azure side. Requires the higher-resolution architecture diagram referenced but not included in the deck.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["architecture", "performance", "nfr"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R006",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "The repository README states plainly that auth is a dev-grade shortcut: the BFF hands the Open Loyalty token to the browser and it is kept in localStorage. This is incompatible with the security posture the tender requires (BRD §12.5 — ISO 27001 or SOC 2 Type II, IAM and encryption evidence) and with the CIAM/SSO model in BR-DI-007.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "high",
      "mitigation": "Session handling must be redesigned around the CIAM platform with server-side token custody behind an httpOnly session cookie. Treat the current pattern as prototype-only and confirm it is not carried into the Phase 1 build.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["security", "auth", "ciam"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R007",
      "category": "operational",
      "description": "The Phase 1 parking benefit is fulfilled by a physical, printed coupon issued through the loyalty channel (BR-P1-025) — the only mandatory launch benefit that cannot complete inside the app. Issuance, carriage, presentation and monthly-cap tracking are all undefined, and the mechanism is discarded in Phase 2 when gantry integration replaces it.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "likelihood": "high",
      "mitigation": "Define the coupon lifecycle end-to-end with NTUC Club operations before designing the in-app entitlement view. Design the member's mental model around 'entitlement remaining this month' so the Phase 2 switch to automatic gantry application does not change what the member understands.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["parking", "phase-1-workaround", "operations"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R008",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "NARROWED by the RA-05 desk pass (2026-08-17). The general form of this risk — that the engine may not express member-facing needs — is largely refuted: the spec models wallets, 31 webhook events, segments and custom fields richly, and the voucher wallet (f6) is cheaper than assumed. The mock's thinness was never evidence about the engine. What survives are the two originally named examples, both confirmed as real: expiry-cohort visibility (f4) is carried by exactly one route in 195 and that route is deprecated (see R014), and multi-redemption (BR-P1-017) has no engine mechanism at all (see I016). A third constraint emerged: the mock actively misleads on f4 — serializeCustomerStatus hardcodes pointsExpiringNextMonth to 0 and omits pointsExpiringBreakdown entirely, so anyone building against it would wrongly conclude a shadow ledger is required.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Do not treat the mock as evidence of engine behaviour in either direction. Run the live-instance verification (RA-05b) against the 10-item test list in 01-research/findings/RA-05-openloyalty-capability-desk-pass.gen-e2.research before member-facing designs promise any of f4, f6 or BR-P1-017. Residual uncertainty is now concentrated in runtime semantics — consumption order, refund effects, campaign configuration — rather than in API surface.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["open-loyalty", "engine-constraint", "integration"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R009",
      "category": "commercial",
      "description": "Tenant participation basis is recorded as 'opt-in vs mandated — to be confirmed' (BRD §6), and tenant participation agreements are a named dependency (§17.2). A member's ability to earn depends entirely on which tenants are live at launch. A sparse footprint at go-live undermines the acquisition and activation KPIs regardless of how good the member surface is.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Obtain the confirmed launch tenant list and its expected density per property. Design the member surface to make participating tenants discoverable, so members know where their card works rather than discovering where it does not.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["tenants", "footprint", "kpi"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R010",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "NFR-009 requires the card, enrolment and redemption flows to be optimised for use 'at physical points of presence' — indoor tills in clubhouses, a water park and a resort, at peak on public and school holidays. NFR-007 asks for graceful degradation so points-earning events are not lost, and the tenant scanner is given an offline queue in the solutioning deck. No equivalent offline guarantee is stated for the member's side: if the card cannot render without connectivity, the member cannot transact.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Establish an explicit offline requirement for the membership card and voucher wallet, and verify it against real conditions at a representative site. The repository already carries vite-plugin-pwa/Workbox, so an offline app shell exists — what is missing is the guarantee that the card payload itself is cached and valid. RA-10 finding A11Y-V4 (2026-08-17) sharpens this: /api is cached NetworkFirst with a 5-minute expiry and the member surface shows no staleness or connectivity signal, so at a till a stale balance is presently indistinguishable from a live one.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["offline", "card", "nfr"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R011",
      "category": "regulatory-compliance",
      "description": "Every clause of the BRD requires a Compliance / Partially Compliant / Non-Compliant / Not Applicable verdict with mandatory substantiating remarks for anything other than 'C'. Member-surface requirements where the design is still open — notably BR-P1-017 (multiple redemptions per transaction), BR-P1-018 (dual-channel redemption) and BR-P1-025 (parking coupon) — cannot be answered 'C' with confidence until discovery closes those gaps.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Track which member-facing requirements discovery must resolve before the compliance response is finalised, and flag those to the bid team as a dependency rather than discovering the conflict at submission.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["compliance", "tender-response"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R012",
      "category": "operational",
      "description": "Accessibility debt will be multiplied by the build. RA-10 (2026-08-17) found the existing four screens contain exactly one ARIA attribute (aria-hidden on tab icons), zero autocomplete attributes, no focus token, no live regions and no route-change focus management — and that several apparent passes are library defaults rather than intent (aria-current comes free from react-router NavLink; 2.4.1 passes only because <nav> happens to follow <main>). Nine further features are due in roughly 15 weeks. Establishing the patterns now is cheap; retrofitting them across twelve features is not.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "high",
      "mitigation": "Fix the pattern-level defects before feature work starts, not the instances: add focus, disabled and semantic tokens; establish an aria-live convention; make autocomplete mandatory on every input; add route-change focus management and per-route titles. Pair with the conformance-target decision in I013 so the team is building to an agreed standard rather than a guess.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["accessibility", "technical-debt", "build-readiness"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R013",
      "category": "commercial",
      "description": "BRD §4 commits to 'a branded loyalty platform under the NTUC Club identity', and RA-09 (2026-08-17) confirms no NTUC Club brand asset of any kind is held: no logo, palette, typeface, icon set or tone of voice, and no group design standard in the ingested pack. The member app's current palette is verifiably the Tailwind default indigo/violet/emerald — a framework default with no relationship to the client. This is a Statement-of-Compliance exposure (see R011) as well as a design blocker.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "likelihood": "high",
      "mitigation": "Obtain the brand guidelines as a named dependency (D011) with a target date preceding solution design sign-off. Until they arrive, treat all current colour, type and spacing values as scaffolding to be replaced, and hold the ten contrast constraints recorded in RA-09 §7 ready to apply to the incoming palette.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["brand", "compliance", "design-system"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R014",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "The expiry-cohort breakdown that feature f4 depends on exists in Open Loyalty in exactly the right shape (CustomerStatus.pointsExpiringBreakdown), but RA-05 found its only carrier across all 195 paths is GET /member/{member}/status, marked deprecated: true with no documented successor. Deriving the same view from /member/points is blocked: the Transfer schema exposes value but no remaining amount, and no source states whether spending consumes points FIFO or nearest-expiry-first. If the route is withdrawn, f4 requires an integration-layer shadow ledger.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Confirm with Open Loyalty whether the deprecated status route has a successor and what the point-consumption order is — the first two items on the RA-05 test list, and the two questions the specification cannot answer. Do not commit f4's member-facing design until both are answered. Size the shadow-ledger fallback (escalation E3) so the cost of the bad branch is known rather than discovered.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["open-loyalty", "expiry", "engine-constraint"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R015",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "BR-P1-012 requires that a refund where points have already been redeemed be flagged for manual adjustment. RA-05 found no engine equivalent — Open Loyalty offers only allowNegativeBalance. The exception queue, the CX workflow that works it, and the member-facing explanation are all unscoped. Separately, PointsWereCanceled carries no actionCause, so explaining any reversal in the member's transaction history costs two additional joins per ledger row.",
      "impact": "high",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Treat the refund exception as a designed path in the service blueprint (RA-06), not an edge case. Confirm the CX handling process in stakeholder interviews (RA-04). Decide early whether the member history shows a reason for a reversal, because the join cost is an architecture decision, not a copy decision.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["refund", "failure-path", "open-loyalty"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R016",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "Open Loyalty's own documentation states that cancelling a transaction 'leaves the tier standing' — a refund reverses points but does not revert a tier the refunded spend helped achieve. Neither the BRD nor the solutioning deck addresses this. A member can therefore hold a tier the underlying spend no longer supports, and both the member surface and CX need a defensible account of why.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "likelihood": "high",
      "mitigation": "Confirm the behaviour against the live instance, then make it an explicit product decision rather than an inherited default: either accept it and say nothing, accept it and disclose it in the tier terms, or build reversal in the integration layer. Route through RA-04 (Finance and CX) and RA-06.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["refund", "tiering", "open-loyalty"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R017",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "BR-P1-011's mandated pre-expiry notification exceeds every benchmarked comparator — RA-07 found none of the five publishes one. But NTUC's per-transaction rolling expiry is also the only non-cohort model in the comparator set: three of five use quarterly, monthly or annual cohorts precisely because cohorts are displayable. Rolling per-earning expiry is the hardest shape both to notify on and to render, and RA-05 could not confirm the engine expresses it.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Add expiry shape to the live-instance verification (RA-05b): can per-earning rolling expiry be surfaced to a member, and is a quarterly or monthly cohort view the fallback? Name the cohort view as the design fallback now so f4 has somewhere to land. Links R014 and D005.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["expiry", "notifications", "open-loyalty"]
    },
    {
      "id": "R018",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json carries an empty info.version string. Its provenance is unverified and it may not correspond to the version that will be provisioned for this tenant. Every RA-05 verdict — including the two escalations — rests on it.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "likelihood": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Confirm the tenant's actual Open Loyalty release version and re-check the deprecated-route and multi-redemption findings against it. This is the cheapest item on the RA-05 test list and should be the first question asked when instance access is granted.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["open-loyalty", "provenance", "evidence-quality"]
    }
  ],

  "assumptions": [
    {
      "id": "A001",
      "category": "operational",
      "description": "Downtown East is included in the Phase 1 launch footprint alongside the 4 Clubhouses, Orchid Country Club and Aranda Country Club.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "high",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Client clarification against issue I001. Proceeding on the majority reading pending an answer — confirmed as the working assumption with the discovery lead on 2026-08-17.",
      "evidence": "BRD §2, §5.3 and §15 all include DE; §8 alone excludes it. Three sections against one.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "DE excluded from Phase 1, per BRD §8", "pros": "Smaller launch footprint; §8 heads the Phase 1 requirements chapter and could be read as authoritative on Phase 1 scope", "cons": "Contradicts the go-live milestone in §15 which names DE explicitly; DE is the largest property and its exclusion would materially change the acquisition model" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["scope", "footprint"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A002",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "Families, PMEs and DINKs/Silvers are the correct primary segments for the Member platform, and their needs differ enough to warrant distinct design consideration.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "high",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "The test is now designed, not merely planned. The RA-01 screener (01-research/discussion-guides/RA-01-participant-screener.md, 2026-08-17) recruits against a behavioural grid and records the three named segments as a post-hoc classification with an explicit 'fits none of the three' value. Where behaviour and demography conflict, behaviour wins and the imbalance is reported as evidence here — so the mismatch becomes visible in the recruitment table before any interview runs. JTBD interviews (RA-02) then test needs difference.",
      "evidence": "Stated by client in BRD §3.2 with no supporting research. NFR-010 corroborates the family/multi-generational emphasis.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "Segment by visit behaviour (frequency and property mix) rather than demographics", "pros": "Behavioural segmentation maps directly onto the earn/tier mechanics the programme actually operates; now operationalised as the RA-01 recruitment frame", "cons": "No behavioural data exists yet — this is precisely what the programme is being built to capture" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["segments", "research"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A003",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "SingPass/MyInfo will be the dominant enrolment path in practice, justifying its treatment as the baseline and the design investment in MyInfo pre-fill. CONTRADICTED IN ITS JUSTIFICATION by RA-07 (2026-08-17): no benchmarked Singapore loyalty programme uses SingPass for enrolment. Social and email sign-in are the market norm. The case for SingPass therefore rests on the Athena union-verification requirement and identity assurance, not on prevailing Singapore practice — and it should be argued on that basis. Knock-on: R001's impact may be lower than logged, since the non-SingPass paths are what comparable programmes actually run on.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "high",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Re-word the RA-02 enrolment-path question to present a genuine choice rather than a presumed default. RA-01 screener question S20 supplies the first behavioural SingPass-usage evidence at screening stage, ahead of RA-02. Benchmarking (RA-07) is complete and contradicts the practice-based justification.",
      "evidence": "Client states SingPass is 'consistent with NTUC's current standard' (BR-P1-020, BRD §17.1). No usage data provided. RA-07: zero of five benchmarked SG programmes use SingPass for loyalty enrolment; CapitaStar at 1.8M members still enrols at staffed counters and kiosks.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "Social and email sign-in dominate; SingPass is a minority path used mainly for union verification", "pros": "Lower friction for casual visitors; reduces exposure to the SNDGO approval dependency (R001)", "cons": "Weakens identity assurance and complicates the deterministic match to NTUC HQ that drives Tier 2 auto-placement" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["identity", "enrolment"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A004",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "Members will readily present a QR code on their phone at a tenant till, and tenant staff will reliably scan it — the Changi Rewards behaviour transfers to NTUC Club's properties and tenant mix.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "high",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Contextual inquiry at a representative tenant till (RA-03), sharpened to record whether members present unprompted when the only reward is 0.5%. RA-01 screener question S21 (do you show anything on a phone at a till?) supplies pre-concept evidence at screening stage. Competitor benchmarking (RA-07) is complete.",
      "evidence": "BR-P1-001 explicitly models the capture mechanism on the Changi Rewards App. BRD §4 names Changi Rewards as the usability and feature-maturity benchmark. PARTIALLY CONTRADICTED by RA-07 (2026-08-17): Changi's presentation habit is underwritten by 9% GST absorption gated on the same card scan — roughly 16x the points value — so the behaviour may not transfer to a 0.5% points-only proposition. CapitaStar has removed the presentation moment entirely (receiptless since 8 July 2025); FRx mandates it and draws public complaints for it. The market holds three incompatible models of the card moment and BR-P1-001 picked the most demanding.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "Members forget or decline to present at the moment of payment, and earning depends on retrospective capture", "pros": "Strengthened by RA-07: four of five comparators operate a recovery path (Changi 7-day receipt webform, SAFRA 14 days, HomeTeamNS receipt-scan-as-primary, CapitaStar removed the failure mode). The one that does not is FRx, the one members complain about", "cons": "Retrospective capture is not in Phase 1 scope and would be new work against a fixed date — see I011, now raised to major" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["earn", "card", "behaviour"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A005",
      "category": "commercial",
      "description": "The earn and redemption economics — $1 = 1 point, 1,000 points for a $5 or $10 voucher — are competitive enough to drive the Phase 1 first-redemption KPI.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "high",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Competitor benchmarking (RA-07) complete 2026-08-17. Remaining: test perceived value of all three options in JTBD interviews (RA-02) — flat 0.5%, flat 1%, and the Tier 2 redemption-value uplift — plus Finance liability modelling in RA-04.",
      "evidence": "BRD §11.3 acknowledges Scenario 1 'may feel less compelling against comparable mall/attraction loyalty programmes' — the client already suspects this. PREMISE CONTRADICTED by RA-07: against malls and the airport, 0.5% is the market rate (Changi entry 0.5%, CapitaStar 0.5% flat, FRx Basic 0.5%). The genuine gap is against the two clubhouse estates NTUC Club structurally resembles — HomeTeamNS at 1% and SAFRA at 2% — though both charge subscriptions NTUC Club will not. The decisive finding is structural rather than numeric: in every tiered comparator, 0.5% is the rate a customer escapes, at S$4,000 (Changi Gold) or $8,000 (FRx Elite). NTUC's Tier 2 threshold is the lowest in the set at $1,500 and buys no yield increase at all.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "0.5% at Tier 1, 1.0% at Tier 2 delivered through redemption value ($5 vs $10 per 1,000 points) — RA-07's recommended option", "pros": "Costs less than a flat 1%; is exactly what BR-P1-007 prescribes (differentiate by redemption value, not earn rate); is demonstrated in-market by FRx, the only SG programme differentiating a tier this way; and is already specified in BRD §11.4, merely assigned to Tier 3 in Phase 2. Resolves I004 and R004 together", "cons": "Depends on FRx figures currently sourced secondarily — see D013. Adds a second conversion rate to the member's mental model and to the admin-configurable table" },
        { "description": "Adopt Scenario 2 (1% yield) for launch to maximise acquisition and first redemption, revisiting after Phase 1", "pros": "BRD's own recommendation if faster acquisition is prioritised; better aligned to Phase 1 KPIs; matches HomeTeamNS", "cons": "Approximately doubles redemption cost and points liability, and still leaves Tier 2 with no differentiator" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["economics", "rebate-yield", "kpi"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A006",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "Union members will perceive automatic Tier 2 placement as a meaningful benefit of union membership rather than as a default state.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "medium",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "JTBD interviews with union members specifically (RA-02), testing the tier proposition against R004.",
      "evidence": "Implied by the programme design (BR-P1-005) and by NTUC Club's positioning. No direct evidence. Benchmark context from RA-07 (2026-08-17): the historical PAssion–CapitaStar tie-up is the closest documented Singapore precedent for conferring affinity status on a points programme, and it chose a 1.5x earn multiplier as the differentiator — precisely the lever BR-P1-007 removes.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "Union members treat Tier 2 as an entitlement they already have, and recognition must come from somewhere else", "pros": "Would redirect design effort toward benefits that feel earned rather than conferred", "cons": "Conflicts with the stated tier structure; would need Marketing's agreement" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["tiering", "union-members"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A007",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "The existing apps/pwa codebase is the starting point for the Phase 1 Member platform rather than a throwaway prototype, and its architecture (React 18 + Vite + TypeScript, BFF to Open Loyalty) carries forward.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "medium",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Confirm with the delivery lead. Note the tension with the solutioning deck, which proposes Node.js/NestJS for backend services and Flutter for a mobile app alongside the ReactJS PWA — see issue I010.",
      "evidence": "The repository exists, is structured as the six services the solution describes, and vendors the real Open Loyalty OpenAPI spec as source of truth.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "The repository is a demonstrator built for the pitch, and Phase 1 is built fresh on the proposed NestJS stack", "pros": "Aligns with the tech stack actually proposed to the client", "cons": "Discards working integration code against a 15-week timeline" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["codebase", "stack"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A008",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "Existing member and transaction data will be made available by NTUC HQ systems for migration in a reasonable format, per BRD §17.1.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "medium",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Stakeholder interview with NTUC HQ / Group IT (RA-04). Member-surface relevance: migrated members may arrive with a pre-existing balance and tier and must not be treated as new enrolments.",
      "evidence": "Stated as an assumption by the client in BRD §17.1 — an assumption the client has flagged as their own, not validated.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "No usable historical data exists; every member enrols fresh at launch", "pros": "Simplifies the enrolment path considerably — one flow, no migration state", "cons": "Loses the head start on the database-growth KPI and the goodwill of recognising existing customers" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["migration", "enrolment"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A009",
      "category": "regulatory-compliance",
      "description": "WCAG 2.2 Level AA is the applicable accessibility conformance standard for the Member platform.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "high",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Confirm with NTUC Club, or replace with whatever standard they are bound to. Route through stakeholder interviews (RA-04). See I013.",
      "evidence": "Assumed by RA-10 in the total absence of any contracted requirement. RA-09 confirms BRD §12.3, titled 'Usability & Accessibility', contains three usability NFRs and zero accessibility requirements, and that there are zero matches for WCAG, screen reader, assistive or disability across all four extracted tender documents.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "A different or higher standard applies — e.g. an NTUC Enterprise group policy, or a Singapore public-sector digital service standard NTUC Club has adopted", "pros": "Would settle the question definitively and may already exist unshared", "cons": "A higher bar discovered late is expensive; this is precisely why it should be asked now rather than at UAT" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["accessibility", "compliance", "standard"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A010",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "Open Loyalty's Management and Integration API labels reflect real token scoping — that is, a member JWT cannot reach admin routes.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "high",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Test directly against the live instance (RA-05b test item T7). Cheap to run, and the answer is binary.",
      "evidence": "Inferred from the OpenAPI spec's own labelling. Never tested. The in-repo mock does not honour the distinction — it lets a member token buy rewards — so the codebase currently embodies the opposite assumption.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "The labels are documentation only and member tokens can reach Management routes", "pros": "Would simplify the client-side redemption flow considerably", "cons": "Would be a security finding for the ISO 27001 / SOC 2 evidence pack (BRD §12.5), not a convenience. Compounds R006, since the current build hands the engine token to the browser" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["open-loyalty", "security", "auth"]
    },
    {
      "id": "A011",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "An assisted or counter-based enrolment path is not required — BR-P1-020's five digital doors (SingPass, Google, Apple, email, mobile OTP) are sufficient for all members including the Silvers sub-segment.",
      "confidence": "assumption",
      "impact": "medium",
      "status": "pending",
      "validationPlan": "Raise directly in stakeholder interviews (RA-04) as a scope question, and test the underlying premise in RA-08 usability testing with the 60+ cohort. Relevant to H003.",
      "evidence": "Implied by BR-P1-020 listing digital paths only. Contradicted in market practice by RA-07: CapitaStar still operates staffed counters and kiosks at 1.8M members. Every NTUC tenant will already have a scanner under BR-P1-001, so the physical infrastructure for an assisted path largely exists.",
      "alternatives": [
        { "description": "Offer an assisted enrolment path at clubhouse counters", "pros": "The obvious mitigation for the Silvers exposure (H003) and for any SingPass approval slip (R001); infrastructure is largely in place", "cons": "Unscoped Phase 1 work; introduces a staff-training and identity-assurance burden not currently costed" }
      ],
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["enrolment", "accessibility", "scope"]
    }
  ],

  "issues": [
    {
      "id": "I001",
      "category": "operational",
      "description": "The BRD contradicts itself on the Phase 1 launch footprint. §8 states Phase 1 establishes habits 'across participating tenants at multiple locations, outside of Downtown East (DE)'. §2, §5.3 and the §15 go-live milestone all include Downtown East explicitly. DE is the largest property; its inclusion or exclusion materially changes the acquisition model and the tenant onboarding volume.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Raise as a formal clarification with NTUC Club. Working assumption pending answer: DE is included (A001).",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["contradiction", "scope", "clarification"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I002",
      "category": "commercial",
      "description": "BR-P1-007 mandates that both tiers earn at the same underlying rebate yield, with tier differentiation delivered through redemption value and ancillary benefits and explicitly 'not differential earn rates'. BRD §11.5 then grants Tier 1 a 2x and Tier 2 a 3x points multiplier on the first birthday-month transaction — a differential earn rate. BR-P1-024 repeats it.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Seek clarification. Most likely intended reading is that BR-P1-007 governs the base earn rate only and campaign multipliers are exempt — but this must be confirmed, because it determines whether the member surface can advertise Tier 2 as earning more at any point.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["contradiction", "tiering", "earn"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I003",
      "category": "operational",
      "description": "The parking entitlement is stated two ways. BR-P1-025: 'Tier 1: 1 hour/month; Tier 2: 2 hours/month'. BRD §11.5: Tier One '1-hour comp parking, capped at 2/month', Tier Two '2-hour comp parking, capped at 2/month'. The second reads as two coupons per month of 1 and 2 hours respectively — up to 2 and 4 hours — not 1 and 2 hours total.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Clarify with NTUC Club. The member surface must display a remaining entitlement, and cannot do so until the unit (hours per month vs coupons per month) is settled.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["contradiction", "parking", "entitlement"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I004",
      "category": "commercial",
      "description": "The rebate-yield scenario is undecided by the client. 1,000 points buys either a $5 voucher (0.5% yield) or a $10 voucher (1% yield). The BRD requires the conversion table remain admin-configurable precisely because the decision is open. This is the exchange rate the member proposition is built on.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Client decision required at solution design sign-off (Q3 2026), now with a benchmarked recommendation. RA-07 (2026-08-17) establishes three options: flat 0.5% (the Singapore mall/airport entry rate — the BRD's own worry about mall comparability is unfounded), flat 1.0% (matches HomeTeamNS, roughly doubles liability), or 0.5% at Tier 1 rising to 1.0% at Tier 2 through redemption value. The third is recommended: it costs less than a flat 1%, it is the mechanism BR-P1-007 already prescribes, it is demonstrated in-market by FRx, and BRD §11.4 already specifies the uplift — merely assigning it to Tier 3 in Phase 2. It resolves R004 at the same time. Two caveats before client use: the FRx figures underpinning it are secondary-sourced and need primary verification (D013), and member perception is still untested (RA-02). Until resolved, no member-facing copy or layout may hard-code a voucher value.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["open-decision", "economics", "client"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I005",
      "category": "commercial",
      "description": "'Activation rate' is a named Phase 1 KPI with no definition. It could mean first app open after enrolment, first card presentation, first points earned, or first redemption — each implies a different priority on the member surface, and no numeric target is given for it or for any other Phase 1 KPI.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Define with Marketing during stakeholder interviews (RA-04), together with numeric targets for all four Phase 1 KPIs. Without a definition the member surface cannot be designed to move the metric, nor measured against it.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["kpi", "measurement", "definition"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I006",
      "category": "commercial",
      "description": "BRD §11 states that to maintain Tier 2 into the following membership year, members must redeem 1,000 points before the expiry date — flagged 'subject to confirmation'. BR-P1-003 separately anticipates a Year 2+ rule where Tier 2 retention requires a points deduction or renewal spend. If a retention condition applies at launch it is member-visible and must be communicated from day one; if it applies only from Year 2 the surface must be built to introduce it later without surprise.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Confirm with NTUC Club whether any retention condition is live in Phase 1. Design the tier surface so a retention requirement can be introduced without re-architecting the member's understanding of their status.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["tiering", "retention", "open-decision"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I007",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "Redemption initiation is ambiguous. BR-P1-018 permits redemption via the digital channel and at tenant point-of-sale. The solutioning deck's Member: Redemption journey is staff-driven — the member asks in the moment, staff scan the member's card, a voucher is issued and auto-invalidated on use. The existing PWA is member-driven — browse the catalogue, redeem, receive a coupon code. These are different products for the member. BR-P1-017 compounds it by requiring multiple redemptions within a single transaction.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Resolve through contextual inquiry at a tenant till (RA-03) and the service blueprint (RA-06). The answer determines whether the rewards catalogue or the voucher wallet is the primary redemption surface.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["redemption", "journey", "contradiction"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I008",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "The identity-matching exception queue has no member-facing experience. The solutioning deck's enrolment flow resolves each member to 'link, create or exception queue' against Salesforce and NTUC HQ. A member who lands in the exception queue is stranded at the point of highest acquisition intent, and nothing in the BRD or the deck describes what they see, how long they wait, or how they recover.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Design the exception path explicitly as part of the enrolment flow (f2). Requires knowing the expected exception rate and the CX team's handling process — a stakeholder interview target (RA-04).",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["enrolment", "identity", "failure-path"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I009",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "BRD §13.3 lists 'Mobile App (Vendor to propose) — potential shared identity / unified access layer, architectural decision pending' as a Phase 1 item. The solutioning deck's tech stack names both ReactJS PWA and Flutter for a mobile app; its System Landscape diagram labels the member channel 'Member App — iOS & Android'. The repository is a PWA. What is actually delivered to members at go-live — installable PWA, native apps, or both — is unsettled, and it changes the enrolment, card and notification designs.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Confirm the channel decision with the delivery lead and NTUC Club before enrolment and card design proceeds. Push notification delivery (f11) in particular differs materially between PWA and native on iOS.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["channel", "architecture", "open-decision"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I010",
      "category": "regulatory-compliance",
      "description": "Two documents required for the tender response are not in the ingested pack: the NTUC Club IT Outsource Security Requirement Checklist v3.xlsx (BRD §12.5) and the higher-resolution architecture diagram referenced by the solutioning deck. The solutioning deck's diagrams are also absent from the text extract, so the architecture is known only from its labels.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "severity": "minor",
      "resolution": "Request all from the bid team. RA-09 (2026-08-17) adds a third absent input: NTUC Club brand guidelines and any NTUC Enterprise group design system, now tracked as dependency D011. Also extend the security-checklist question to cover where screener and research personal data may be stored (see D012). If architecture detail becomes blocking, read the relevant pages of solutioning.pdf as images rather than text.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["missing-input", "compliance"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I011",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "The solutioning deck's Member: Earn journey mentions 'upload receipt' as an earn path alongside scanning the card at payment. No BRD requirement covers receipt upload in Phase 1 — BR-P1-001 describes only tenant-side QR scanning. If receipt upload is being offered it is unscoped member-facing work; if it is not, the journey description overstates Phase 1.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "RAISED from minor to major by RA-07 (2026-08-17). Four of five benchmarked Singapore comparators operate a recovery path when the card is not presented — Changi a 7-day receipt webform, SAFRA 14 days, HomeTeamNS receipt-scan-as-primary, CapitaStar having removed the failure mode altogether. The single comparator without one is FRx, and it is the one drawing public complaints. NTUC Phase 1 currently matches FRx. Confirm with the delivery lead whether receipt upload is in Phase 1 scope, and raise it as a stakeholder question in RA-04. Directly relevant to A004.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["scope", "earn", "clarification"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I012",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "NTUC member Tier 2 auto-placement is 'subject to eligibility rules to be finalised' (BR-P1-005). Whether all union members qualify, or only some class of them, is undetermined — and it decides what a large, identifiable group of members sees on their first screen after enrolment.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Confirm with NTUC HQ and Marketing (RA-04). The enrolment success state (f2, f7) cannot be designed while it is open.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["tiering", "union-members", "open-decision"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I013",
      "category": "regulatory-compliance",
      "description": "No accessibility conformance standard is specified anywhere in the tender. BRD §12.3 is titled 'Usability & Accessibility' and contains three usability NFRs and zero accessibility requirements. RA-09 (2026-08-17) found zero matches for WCAG, screen reader, assistive or disability across all four extracted tender documents. This is a public programme that will deliberately recruit a 60+ cohort and serve multi-generational families, with no agreed standard to build or test against.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Agree the conformance target with NTUC Club — RA-10 assumed WCAG 2.2 Level AA (logged as A009) and that assumption needs confirming or replacing. Route through RA-04, which has no trigger and can start immediately. Blocks RA-09 part 2 and should be settled before component work begins.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["accessibility", "compliance", "missing-requirement"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I014",
      "category": "user-market",
      "description": "The member surface is dark-first with no light mode, and a scannable QR code needs dark modules on a light field. Either the membership card view breaks the theme or the programme is not dark-first. This lands on f1, the single most-used screen in the programme, and it is a design-system input rather than an implementation detail.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Settle as part of the design-language decision (RA-09 part 2), not during build. Note that the constraint holds regardless of what the NTUC Club brand guidelines turn out to say, so the question can be framed for the client now: does the card invert, or does the app go light?",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["design-system", "card", "theme"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I015",
      "category": "operational",
      "description": "The four programme surfaces share no design language. RA-09 (2026-08-17) found apps/pwa, admin, merchant and studio each declare unrelated palettes under different naming conventions with no shared package. The member app is the only dark one — and it meets the merchant app across the same counter.",
      "impact": "medium",
      "severity": "minor",
      "resolution": "Decide whether the member and tenant-facing surfaces should read as one system. This is part of the blocked inherit-vs-define decision (RA-09 part 2, D011), not a tidiness issue — and it is cheaper to answer before four codebases diverge further.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["design-system", "cross-surface", "consistency"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I016",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "ESCALATED 2026-08-17. BR-P1-017 requires multiple redemptions within a single transaction, and Open Loyalty has no mechanism for it. RA-05 found RedeemCouponFormType accepts one singular couponCode, PostTransaction has no coupon field at all, and the only bulk endpoint concerns fulfilment status and states in its own description that it is non-atomic. Multi-redemption orchestration — including the failure path when redemption 2 of 3 fails after points have been spent — is unbudgeted engineering on both the member and merchant surfaces.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Needs a named owner and a scope decision now, not at build. Three routes: build the orchestration in the integration layer with a compensating-transaction design, ask Open Loyalty whether a supported pattern or roadmap item exists (RA-05b test item T3), or descope BR-P1-017 to one redemption per transaction and record it as Partially Compliant in the Statement of Compliance (see R011). Interacts with I007, since it presumes the redemption initiation model is settled.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["escalation", "open-loyalty", "unbudgeted", "redemption"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I017",
      "category": "data-technical",
      "description": "ESCALATED 2026-08-17. Two of the five notification triggers mandated by BR-P1-002b have no Open Loyalty webhook. RA-05 found 31 events in x-webhooks covering enrolment, tier upgrade (levelMoveUp) and points expiry, but birthday emits only a generic CampaignEffectWasApplied, and lapsed-visit is expressible as a segment while SegmentMembershipDropped is count-only and does not name a member. The solutioning deck defines the Notification Service as webhook-driven only, so as architected it cannot originate two of its own requirements.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Add a scheduler and persisted state to the Notification Service so it can originate these two triggers itself. Cheap at week 1, expensive at week 12 — but it changes the component from stateless to stateful, which is an architecture decision other components depend on. Confirm the webhook inventory against the live instance first (RA-05b), since the spec's provenance is unverified (R018).",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["escalation", "notifications", "architecture", "open-loyalty"]
    },
    {
      "id": "I018",
      "category": "commercial",
      "description": "Tier 2 qualifies at $1,500 annual spend — the lowest first-upgrade threshold in the benchmarked comparator set, against Changi Gold at S$4,000 and FRx Elite at $8,000 — while conferring no economic uplift whatsoever (BR-P1-007). RA-07 found no Singapore comparator running a named status tier with neither an earn nor a redemption differential. Combined with union auto-placement, Tier 2 will be both the majority tier and the emptiest.",
      "impact": "high",
      "severity": "major",
      "resolution": "Three coherent options, one of which must be chosen: add economic content (the RA-07 recommendation under I004 — redemption-value uplift), raise the threshold so the tier means something to reach, or drop the 'Premium' framing and present Tier 2 as a benefits bundle rather than a status. Doing none of them is the current path. Test with union members in RA-02 (H002) and model liability with Finance in RA-04.",
      "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]",
      "status": "open",
      "dateRaised": "2026-08-17",
      "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17",
      "tags": ["tiering", "proposition", "benchmarked"]
    }
  ],

  "dependencies": [
    { "id": "D001", "category": "regulatory-compliance", "description": "SNDGO/GovTech approval and API access for SingPass/MyInfo integration (BRD §17.2).", "type": "external", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC — must precede solution design sign-off, Q3 2026]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["identity", "critical-path"] },
    { "id": "D002", "category": "data-technical", "description": "NTUC HQ (Athena) membership verification interface for union-status resolution and Tier 2 auto-placement, plus real-time synchronisation of NTUC membership status (BRD §5.1, BR-DI-003).", "type": "external", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["identity", "union-members"] },
    { "id": "D003", "category": "data-technical", "description": "Bi-directional Salesforce CRM integration for unified member profile, consent records and campaign orchestration (BR-P1-002a).", "type": "external", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["crm", "consent"] },
    { "id": "D004", "category": "data-technical", "description": "Marketing automation platform — not named in either document — required for expiry, enrolment, tier-upgrade, birthday and lapsed-visit notifications (BR-P1-002b, BR-DI-006). Feature f11 cannot be designed until the platform and its channel capabilities are known.", "type": "external", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["notifications", "unnamed-system"] },
    { "id": "D005", "category": "data-technical", "description": "Access to a real Open Loyalty instance (not the in-repo mock) plus an expert contact for the four questions the specification cannot answer: the successor to the deprecated status route, the point-consumption order (FIFO vs nearest-expiry), whether any supported multi-redemption pattern exists, and the tenant's actual release version. URGENCY RAISED 2026-08-17: this is now the single blocker on RA-05b, on RA-06 (whose failure paths cannot be drawn without it), and on sizing escalations I016 and I017. Acceptance criteria are the 10-item test list in 01-research/findings/RA-05-openloyalty-capability-desk-pass.gen-e2.research — request access with that defined purpose attached. See R008, R014, R018.", "type": "external", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC — URGENT: blocks RA-05b, RA-06 and two live escalations]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["open-loyalty", "engine", "urgent", "critical-path"] },
    { "id": "D006", "category": "commercial", "description": "Tenant participation agreements at the launch properties, on a basis (opt-in vs mandated) still to be confirmed (BRD §6, §17.2).", "type": "external", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC — ahead of go-live]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["tenants", "footprint"] },
    { "id": "D007", "category": "commercial", "description": "NTUC Club decision on the rebate-yield scenario, which sets the member-visible conversion table (issue I004).", "type": "internal-client", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "Solution design sign-off, Q3 2026", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["open-decision", "economics"] },
    { "id": "D008", "category": "commercial", "description": "NTUC Club decision on tier qualification, retention and downgrade rules, and on NTUC member eligibility for auto-placement (issues I006, I012).", "type": "internal-client", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "Solution design sign-off, Q3 2026", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["tiering", "open-decision"] },
    { "id": "D009", "category": "data-technical", "description": "CIAM/SSO platform provisioning (Entra External ID per the solutioning deck) covering SingPass/MyInfo, Google, Apple, email and mobile OTP (BR-DI-007).", "type": "internal", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["identity", "ciam"] },
    { "id": "D010", "category": "operational", "description": "Participant recruitment support from NTUC Club for member research. SPLIT from the original combined entry on 2026-08-17 — till access is a different ask to different people on a different date and is now D014. Three specific requirements, per the RA-01 screener: (1) channel access rather than participant selection — NTUC Club opens the channels, PALO IT selects, so the sample is not curated; (2) contactable lapsed-member records, the highest-value recruitment asset the client holds and never offered unprompted; (3) community and senior-centre introductions, since the 60+ and lapsed cells cannot be sourced from NTUC Club's active member list or on-site intercept by construction. The RA-01 instrument is drafted and blocked on this dependency alone.", "type": "internal-client", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC — blocks RA-01, and through it RA-02 and RA-08]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["research-access", "recruitment"] },
    { "id": "D011", "category": "operational", "description": "NTUC Club brand guidelines and any NTUC Enterprise group design system — logo, palette, typeface, icon set, tone of voice. RA-09 (2026-08-17) confirms none is held. Blocks RA-09 part 2, the inherit-vs-define decision, and all member-surface visual design. See R013 and I010.", "type": "internal-client", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC — must precede solution design sign-off, Q3 2026]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["brand", "design-system", "missing-input"] },
    { "id": "D012", "category": "regulatory-compliance", "description": "NTUC Club and PALO IT legal position on research data handling, needed before the RA-01 screener can be fielded and independently of D010. Five open points: who controls screener data and whether it may flow into Salesforce or the marketing platform respondents did not consent to; whether DNC obligations apply to outbound research recruitment (this one cannot be designed around, since the phone route is required for the 60+ cohort); whether lapsed members may be contacted and on what basis; incentive and gift policy for union-linked recipients; and whether the unseen IT Outsource Security Requirement Checklist (I010) constrains where research personal data may be stored. Cross-references BR-P1-022.", "type": "internal-client", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC — blocks RA-01 fielding]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["pdpa", "research-access", "legal"] },
    { "id": "D013", "category": "commercial", "description": "Primary-source verification of the FRx Basic/Elite redemption-value split ($2.50 vs $5.00 per 1,000 points) and the $8,000 Elite threshold. RA-07 sourced these secondarily because the FRx site is JS-rendered and its T&C reserves conversion to operator discretion — and they are the load-bearing evidence for the recommended I004 option. Low effort: install the FRx app or email FRHelp@frasersproperty.com. Blocks client-facing use of the recommendation, not the recommendation itself.", "type": "internal", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN — research]", "targetDate": "[TBC — before the I004 recommendation goes to the client]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["benchmarking", "evidence-quality", "low-effort"] },
    { "id": "D014", "category": "operational", "description": "Site access to 2–3 representative tenant tills across different property types (clubhouse F&B, Downtown East retail, an attraction), including at least one at peak, for contextual inquiry. SPLIT from D010 on 2026-08-17 — a different ask to different people on a different date. Depends on the launch tenant list being confirmed (D006, I001).", "type": "internal-client", "owner": "[HUMAN TO ASSIGN]", "targetDate": "[TBC — blocks RA-03, and through it RA-06]", "status": "pending", "dateRaised": "2026-08-17", "dateUpdated": "2026-08-17", "tags": ["research-access", "contextual-inquiry", "tenants"] }
  ],

  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "id": "H001",
      "statement": "We believe that a member standing at a tenant till will be able to reach their membership card within one action of opening the app, because at that moment the card is the only thing they need — but we have no evidence about where members expect to find it or how long the queue tolerates.",
      "successCriteria": "In contextual inquiry and usability testing, members reach a scannable card in one action from a cold app start, in under 3 seconds, in at least 9 of 10 attempts — including with degraded connectivity.",
      "testMethod": "prototype-test",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "high",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "learnings": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s1"],
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "H002",
      "statement": "We believe that union members auto-placed into Tier 2 will perceive the tier as meaningful recognition, because its ancillary benefits (day passes, D'Resort discount, early-bird booking, extended parking) carry real value — even though Tier 2 earns at the same rate as Tier 1.",
      "successCriteria": "In JTBD interviews, union members articulate at least one Tier 2 benefit unprompted and describe the tier as something they would not want to lose. If they instead describe it as automatic or meaningless, the hypothesis fails and the tier proposition needs rework.",
      "testMethod": "user-interview",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "high",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s3"],
      "learnings": "",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "H003",
      "statement": "We believe that older members (the Silvers sub-segment) will complete SingPass enrolment on a mobile web app unassisted, because SingPass is broadly adopted across Singapore age groups — but the combination of SingPass redirect, PWA install and QR presentation has not been tested with this group.",
      "successCriteria": "In moderated usability testing, at least 8 of 10 participants aged 60+ complete enrolment and reach a displayed membership card without assistance.",
      "testMethod": "prototype-test",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "high",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s2", "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s7", "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/01-research/findings/RA-10-heuristic-accessibility.gen-e2.he"],
      "learnings": "Not yet tested, but RA-10 (2026-08-17) supplies concrete test targets rather than a general concern: zero autocomplete attributes on any input (the defect most likely to block this cohort), roughly 32px 'Redeem' and 16px 'See all' touch targets, 11.5px tab labels, and a dark-only theme. RA-01 recruitment specifies that at least 2 of the 4 participants aged 60+ must show low device signal — four digitally fluent 60-year-olds would meet the number and defeat the purpose. Note also A011: no assisted enrolment path currently exists as a fallback if this fails.",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "H004",
      "statement": "We believe that members will accept a 12-month rolling points expiry without loss of trust if they are shown which points lapse and when, in advance, because the objection is to surprise rather than to expiry itself.",
      "successCriteria": "In interviews and concept testing, members shown an expiry horizon describe the rule as fair; members shown a bare balance and then a silent deduction describe it as unfair. The difference between the two conditions is the evidence.",
      "testMethod": "user-interview",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "high",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s4"],
      "learnings": "",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "H005",
      "statement": "We believe that members will decide what to redeem before they reach the counter rather than asking staff in the moment, because browsing a catalogue is a considered act and paying is a hurried one — which would make the in-app voucher wallet, not the staff-scanned flow, the primary redemption surface.",
      "successCriteria": "In contextual inquiry at a tenant till, the majority of observed redemptions are member-initiated ahead of payment. If staff-initiated redemption dominates, the primary surface inverts and issue I007 resolves the other way.",
      "testMethod": "contextual-inquiry",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "high",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s5"],
      "learnings": "",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "H006",
      "statement": "We believe that a parking entitlement fulfilled as a physical printed coupon will be claimed and used at meaningful rates, because parking is a genuine cost at these properties — but a benefit that requires collecting a piece of paper may simply be abandoned.",
      "successCriteria": "In interviews, members describe a plausible path from seeing the entitlement in-app to using it at the barrier. Redemption intent is expressed by the majority. Operational feasibility is separately confirmed with NTUC Club operations.",
      "testMethod": "user-interview",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "medium",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s6"],
      "learnings": "",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "H007",
      "statement": "We believe that families will operate one membership per household in practice, with a single adult holding the card and earning on the whole group's spend, because the spend is pooled and the card is presented by whoever pays.",
      "successCriteria": "In JTBD interviews with family visitors, the majority describe a single-holder pattern. If members instead expect to link accounts or pool points across a household, that is unscoped Phase 1 work and must be raised immediately.",
      "testMethod": "user-interview",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "medium",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s7"],
      "learnings": "",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "H008",
      "statement": "We believe that at the 0.5% rebate yield (1,000 points for a $5 voucher) the first-redemption KPI is still achievable, because members value recognition and convenience alongside cash-back value — but $1,000 of spend for $5 may read as not worth pursuing.",
      "successCriteria": "In interviews, members shown both scenarios describe the 0.5% figure as worth working toward. Competitor benchmarking places it within the range of comparable Singapore mall and attraction programmes.",
      "testMethod": "user-interview",
      "status": "partially-supported",
      "impact": "high",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s8", "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/01-research/findings/RA-07-competitor-benchmarking.gen-e2.research"],
      "learnings": "The benchmarking half of the success criterion is MET (RA-07, 2026-08-17): 0.5% sits squarely within the range of comparable Singapore mall and airport programmes — Changi entry 0.5%, CapitaStar 0.5% flat, FRx Basic 0.5% — and $1,000 of spend for a $5 voucher is the same threshold a Changi Member-tier customer faces. The member-perception half remains untested, and now has three conditions to test in RA-02 rather than two (see H010). One caveat that materially qualifies any 'we match Changi' claim: a Changi member's real benefit is 9% GST absorption gated on the same card scan, roughly 16x the points value.",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": "2026-08-17"
    },
    {
      "id": "H009",
      "statement": "We believe that members will treat channel-level consent as a control worth setting rather than a formality to dismiss, because the programme's value depends on being reachable — but consent screens are habitually skipped, and a member who dismisses all channels cannot be told their points are expiring.",
      "successCriteria": "In usability testing, the majority of participants make a deliberate per-channel choice rather than accepting or rejecting all. Participants can locate and change the setting later, unprompted.",
      "testMethod": "prototype-test",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "medium",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief#s9"],
      "learnings": "",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "H010",
      "statement": "We believe that delivering Tier 2's uplift through redemption value (1,000 points = $10 at Tier 2, $5 at Tier 1) will resolve both the rebate-yield decision and the hollow-tier risk at materially lower cost than a flat 1% yield, because it is the mechanism BR-P1-007 already prescribes and the only one demonstrated in the Singapore market.",
      "successCriteria": "In concept testing, members shown the tiered-redemption option describe Tier 2 as worth reaching or keeping, and describe the difference as fair — where the same members shown a flat 0.5% with no tier differential describe Tier 2 as meaningless. Finance's liability model shows the tiered option costing materially less than a flat 1%. If members cannot articulate the difference between $5 and $10 per 1,000 points without prompting, the mechanism is too abstract and the tier needs a concrete benefit instead.",
      "testMethod": "user-interview",
      "status": "untested",
      "impact": "high",
      "conceptRef": "",
      "linkedEvidence": ["gen-e2/discover/customer-app/01-research/findings/RA-07-competitor-benchmarking.gen-e2.research"],
      "learnings": "Derived from RA-07 (2026-08-17). FRx is the only benchmarked Singapore programme differentiating a tier by redemption value rather than earn rate, so the mechanism is in-market but thinly precedented. Test in RA-02 alongside flat 0.5% and flat 1%; model liability with Finance in RA-04. Blocked for client-facing use by D013 until the FRx figures are verified from a primary source. Resolves I004, I018 and R004 together if supported.",
      "dateCreated": "2026-08-17",
      "dateTested": ""
    }
  ]
}
